    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see what you're saying about the material difference. One thing that worries me though, when I was digging through the full text of that 2018 patent, it actually mentions a secondary embodiment that uses ceramic-coated lattices to increase durability. \n\nIf the examiner connects our 'proprietary ceramic' concept to that specific part of the 2018 filing, it might look like we aren't actually offering anything new. Should we address that specific 2018 embodiment in our summary to preemptively separate us, or is it better to just focus on the primary copper-based distinction you already wrote?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I think we should address the ceramic-coated lattice embodiment in the 2018 patent. While it's true that our patent doesn't explicitly disclose a ceramic-coated lattice, the fact that it's a secondary embodiment suggests that the inventors of the 2018 patent didn't consider it a primary aspect of their invention. By highlighting the differences between our ceramic-based material and the copper structures, as well as the novel architecture that integrates our material, we can effectively distinguish our invention from the 2018 patent. We can also argue that the ceramic-coated lattice embodiment is not a direct comparison to our invention, but rather a tangential reference that doesn't impact the overall novelty of our claims. By addressing this potential issue upfront, we can strengthen our position and avoid any potential objections from the examiner.",
      "source": "target",
